          "delegation_parent": "R/C",
          "integration_sources": [],
          "hypothesis_as_declared_by_model": "For sparse obstacles, a first-hit combinatorial recurrence reduces the grid DP to obstacle-pair work, with OpenMP reductions only once each predecessor sum is large enough to amortize team overhead. For denser obstacle sets, a linear-memory row DP uses statically shared chunks whose reset/suffix summaries preserve the row dependency while worksharing each row. This adaptive granularity should suit four CPU cores without allocating an N×N blocked grid.",
          "transformations_as_declared_by_model": {
            "files": [
              "src/solution.cpp"
            ],
            "retained": [
              "Replace the N×N blocked-cell structure with sorted, deduplicated obstacle coordinates and row offsets, keeping auxiliary memory linear in N plus the obstacle count.",
              "Use factorial-based binomial counts and first-hit obstacle DP when the unique obstacle count is at most N; apply static OpenMP reductions to sufficiently long predecessor sums and to the final subtraction.",
              "For denser cases, compute rolling rows; split each row into chunks with blocked-cell reset summaries, scan chunk carries, and statically workshare summary and output passes in a persistent OpenMP region.",
